# Resolution

Fixed in [`299aab30`](https://github.com/justincormack/argmin/commit/299aab30)
(`Bound staging evidence before Raft submission`).

The authenticated staging-evidence path now performs state-aware preflight
inside the existing serialized low-priority evidence-update boundary. One
shared classifier decodes the complete page and returns exactly one of:

- `NewAuthorized`, for a genuinely new page whose actor, intent, evidence
  members, chain position, and publication semantics match current committed
  control-plane authority;
- `ExactReplay`, carrying the original durable apply receipt; or
- a typed rejection.

Exact replays return their original receipt directly from preflight. Semantic
rejections return before OpenRaft submission. Only `NewAuthorized` reaches
`client_write`, so neither replays nor rejected pages advance the Raft log,
increment command-submission metrics, or trigger restart-artifact checkpoint
and filesystem synchronization work.

The control plane now enforces the staging store's authoritative capacity
limit of 64 distinct publication target epochs per exact logical intent
`(pg_id, staging_generation)`. Multiple destination actors may report the same
legitimate target without consuming additional target capacity, but a
credential holder cannot create an unbounded chain by continually selecting
new epochs. Publication evidence must carry both a target and transfer proof,
the target must follow the authorized transition epoch, and the proof's source
epoch cannot exceed the authorized staging source epoch. Tombstones must carry
neither field. The storage node continues to derive the complete imported
proof from the durable staged artifact; destination installation requires all
destination evidence to match the exact requested target and transfer proof.

The same classifier remains the state-machine application boundary, and
snapshot invariant validation independently rejects retained state exceeding
the per-intent target limit. These checks preserve race safety if committed
state changes after preflight and ensure restart cannot admit an over-limit
snapshot.

The integrated regression
`authenticated_staging_evidence_preflight_suppresses_invalid_pages_and_exact_replays`
uses the authenticated Unix RPC and durable single-node OpenRaft host. It
proves that malformed and semantically invalid pages do not advance the log or
durability metrics; exactly 64 distinct targets each advance the log,
submission counter, and checkpoint counter once; the 65th target is rejected
without advancing any of them; direct state-machine application also rejects
that excess page; and repeated exact replay returns the original receipt
without submission or checkpointing.

The validation, evidence, and attack-path sections below describe the
pre-fix implementation and are retained as the historical basis for the
finding.

# Summary
Introduced. The commit exposes staging-evidence publication to storage-node credentials and connects it directly to the normal high-priority Raft command submission path. It does not preflight semantic validity, suppress exact replays before proposal, rate-limit actors, or use the planned low-priority proposal path.
The new storage-node staging-evidence RPC performs only outer page framing, digest, canonicality, actor, and HMAC checks before dispatch. Evidence members are decoded and checked against current transition authority only by the replicated state machine. In the Raft authority implementation, every authenticated request is passed directly to submit_raft_command, which invokes OpenRaft client_write before those semantic checks run. Even when application returns ControlPlaneRaftCommandOutcome::Rejected, finish_submitted_raft_command performs a durable restart-artifact checkpoint before returning the rejection. Exact replays are also deliberately returned as unchanged successful applications, but they have already been submitted through Raft. Consequently, a holder of any configured storage-node credential can repeatedly sign a canonical page containing invalid evidence bytes, unauthorized transition evidence, or an already-retained page. Each request can append and replicate a command, serialize through the shared Raft proposal path, and trigger checkpoint I/O. The per-actor evidence worker guard limits concurrent requests but provides no rate limit or proposal deduplication, so sequential requests can sustain the attack. This can starve legitimate control-plane operations, cause substantial replicated WAL/checkpoint I/O, and eventually exhaust disk on control-plane members.

# Validation
## Rubric
- [x] Confirm a configured storage-node credential can reach the staging-evidence RPC and actor authentication does not require administrator authority; verified by authenticated Unix RPC test and verifier trace.
- [x] Confirm canonical outer pages with semantically invalid or unauthorized member evidence pass outer decoding and fail only in replicated state-machine semantics; verified by source trace, existing transition test, and custom canonical-page reproduction.
- [x] Confirm every admitted request, including repeated rejection and exact replay, enters the normal OpenRaft client-write path before semantic outcome or idempotence handling; verified by log-index advancement and source ordering.
- [x] Confirm rejected/replayed commands cause durable work and that no rate limiter, proposal deduplication, or implemented low-priority path prevents sequential abuse; ten attempts caused ten checkpoints and worker admission was shown to be concurrency-only.
- [x] Exercise the strongest bounded production-representative harness and document limitations; real authenticated Unix RPC and real durable OpenRaft host paths were tested separately, with the lack of a single integrated multi-member stress run recorded as a proof gap.
## Report
Confirmed one authenticated control-plane availability/resource-amplification vulnerability.

Attacker input and preconditions: The input is a canonical, digest-valid metadata-transfer staging-evidence page, wrapped in a StorageStagingEvidencePublish HMAC envelope. Exploitation requires a configured storage-node credential matching the page actor and network/filesystem access to an ordinary authenticated control-plane Unix or TLS listener. It does not require an administrator credential. The verifier derives the expected storage-node principal from the outer page and accepts the configured node credential at crates/storage/src/control_plane/rpc.rs:4748-4854. The focused unmodified test `cargo test -p storage authenticated_unix_staging_evidence_publication_returns_exact_receipt -- --nocapture` passed and exercised the credential-authenticated Unix RPC interface. The project plan also explicitly says this RPC is exposed over authenticated Unix and TLS at plans/multihost-followup-plan.md:493-496. There is not yet a legitimate production outbox caller (plans/multihost-followup-plan.md:497-500), but that does not prevent a credential holder from constructing and sending the documented RPC directly.

Closest controls: RPC admission validates framing and the canonical outer page/digest at crates/storage/src/control_plane/rpc.rs:10427-10443, then authenticates actor, cluster, operation, and HMAC at rpc.rs:4748-4854. It does not decode and authorize individual evidence members against current control-plane state. Authentication replay protection is only a stateless timestamp-window check at crates/storage/src/control_plane_auth.rs:672-702; it has no nonce cache or payload deduplication. Consequently, identical envelopes are acceptable while fresh, and a credential holder can also sign fresh envelopes indefinitely.

Semantic-check location: RPC dispatch passes every admitted page to `apply_metadata_transfer_staging_evidence_page` at crates/storage/src/control_plane/rpc.rs:6099-6114. Member decoding, actor comparison, transition-authority validation, and duplicate-member checks occur later in the replicated state machine at crates/storage/src/control_plane.rs:2822-2855. The existing transition test passed with `cargo test -p storage unavailable_pg_begin_batch_is_atomic_durable_and_requires_whole_batch_replay -- --nocapture`; its assertions show canonical duplicate evidence becoming a committed state-machine rejection at crates/storage/src/control_plane/tests/transitions.rs:830-854, unauthorized evidence being rejected at transitions.rs:1139-1172, and exact retained-page replay returning an unchanged success at transitions.rs:897-902.

Vulnerable sink: The Raft host sends this command through the ordinary `submit_raft_command` path at crates/storage/src/control_plane_raft_host.rs:1250-1266. That path calls the authority submission at control_plane_raft_host.rs:785-792, which serializes submissions on the shared update gate at crates/storage/src/control_plane_raft.rs:3881-3913 and invokes `raft.client_write` at control_plane_raft.rs:4439-4461. OpenRaft applies semantic failures as committed `Rejected` responses rather than aborting the log entry at crates/storage/src/control_plane_raft/state_machine.rs:578-617 and control_plane_command.rs:1895-1915. The existing test `control_plane_openraft_triggered_single_node_client_write_applies_and_rejects` passed and asserts that a rejected command receives the next log index at crates/storage/src/control_plane_raft/tests/cluster.rs:115-129.

Dynamic amplification reproduction: A temporary focused test, included as `custom_test.patch` in the artifact, constructed a canonical and digest-valid but semantically unauthorized staging page, started the real durable single-node OpenRaft authority and `ControlPlaneRaftAuthorityHost`, and submitted the identical page ten times through the host method used by RPC dispatch. Command: `cargo test -p storage validation_repeated_semantically_invalid_staging_pages_commit_and_checkpoint -- --nocapture`. It passed with: `attempts=10 log_index_before=1 log_index_after=11 command_submits_delta=10 checkpoint_stores_delta=10 checkpoint_file_syncs_delta=20 checkpoint_directory_syncs_delta=20 artifact_bytes_before=461 artifact_bytes_after=4421`. Thus every semantic rejection advanced the Raft log, counted as a proposal, performed a durable checkpoint store, caused filesystem sync work, and enlarged the restart artifact. The repository was restored after capturing the patch and output.

Checkpoint ordering and I/O: `finish_submitted_raft_command` checkpoints before distinguishing Applied from Rejected at crates/storage/src/control_plane_raft_host.rs:795-807. Artifact persistence captures the state machine and log store, writes/truncates a temporary artifact, calls `sync_all`, renames it, and syncs the parent directory at crates/storage/src/control_plane_raft.rs:5252-5309 and 8141-8222. This directly explains the measured checkpoint and sync deltas. Exact successful replays hit the unchanged response only in the state machine at control_plane.rs:2757-2776, after this same proposal path has already been entered.

Rate-limit and priority assessment: The evidence worker guard only limits concurrent evidence workers and allows at most one active request per actor at crates/storage/src/control_plane/rpc.rs:7017-7062 and 7422-7487. Its state is removed when a request completes, so it does not limit sequential requests. The unmodified `evidence_confirmation_saturation_does_not_consume_heartbeat_workers` test passed, confirming that this is isolated concurrency admission rather than a request-rate limit. The design document says a separate low-priority proposal queue is intended (plans/multihost-followup-plan.md:852-859) and explicitly records Raft-host low-priority scheduling as unfinished (plans/multihost-followup-plan.md:497-499). No preproposal semantic deduplication or token-bucket control was found.

Counterevidence and proof gaps: Authentication prevents an unauthenticated network attacker and binds the page actor to a configured node credential. The dedicated evidence worker pool preserves ordinary RPC worker capacity against concurrent evidence connections. Production durable Raft startup also launches a checkpoint monitor with 64 MiB/4096-mutation/60-second thresholds at crates/storage/src/control_plane_raft_durability.rs:27-47 and control_plane_raft_peer_bootstrap.rs:600-603; coordinated snapshots and purges can reduce long-term retained-log growth. These controls do not suppress sequential proposals or the per-request checkpoint/fsync path, so they do not falsify the demonstrated control-plane I/O and proposal amplification. The validation did not run one fully integrated authenticated socket directly against a multi-member durable Raft cluster, did not measure starvation of a simultaneous legitimate admin command, and did not run until physical disk exhaustion. Authenticated RPC reachability and durable Raft amplification were instead exercised in separate focused production-path tests. Therefore the core vulnerability is confirmed with high confidence, while the ultimate starvation rate and disk-exhaustion time remain deployment-, storage-, topology-, and checkpoint-scheduling-dependent.

