## Narrative
Attacker story: an attacker who controls an ordinary configured storage node uses its legitimate HMAC credential to submit canonical staging-evidence pages whose members are unauthorized, malformed at the evidence layer, duplicated, or already retained. RPC admission validates framing, digest, actor binding, cluster, operation, freshness, and HMAC, but dispatches the page without current-state semantic validation. The Raft host immediately uses the normal shared proposal path; semantic rejection and exact replay handling occur only after commitment, and the host checkpoints before distinguishing Applied from Rejected. The supplied executable validation submitted the same semantically unauthorized page ten times through the real durable host and observed ten new log indexes, ten proposal submissions, ten checkpoint stores, twenty file syncs, twenty directory syncs, and restart-artifact growth from 461 to 4421 bytes. Authentication, private exposure, connection limits, and a per-actor concurrency guard are meaningful controls, but they do not rate-limit sequential signed requests. The strongest counterevidence is that the project plan calls this an incomplete slice with no production outbox caller and unfinished low-priority scheduling; this lowers likelihood but does not eliminate direct reachability through listeners bound by the production binary. No object data or credential data is disclosed or modified. The reportable impact is shared control-plane availability and durable-storage consumption.
## Controls
- HMAC authentication scoped to cluster, StorageNode actor, ControlPlane target, and StorageStagingEvidencePublish operation
- Actor node ID and incarnation are derived from the canonical outer page
- TLS is required for configured TCP control-plane listeners; Unix sockets are also supported
- Maximum frame size, connection count, and I/O timeout controls exist
- Authentication envelopes have a bounded timestamp freshness window
- Dedicated evidence worker capacity preserves ordinary RPC worker slots
- At most one evidence request per actor may be concurrent
- OpenRaft checkpoint monitoring can snapshot and purge according to WAL-size, mutation-count, and elapsed-time thresholds
- No per-actor request-rate limit, nonce cache, preproposal semantic validation, or preproposal exact-replay suppression exists
- The planned low-priority proposal queue is not implemented in the affected path
## Blindspots
- No repository deployment manifest proves that a real installation exposes the configurable control-plane port beyond an internal network; port 7501 is a documented example.
- The supplied executable amplification test used the real durable single-node authority but did not exercise one authenticated socket against a multi-member cluster.
- The validation did not measure starvation of a simultaneous legitimate command or run until physical disk exhaustion.
- Snapshot and purge scheduling can reduce long-term retained-log growth, so time to disk exhaustion depends on request rate, topology, storage performance, and durability configuration.
- The project plan states that production store opening and outbox publication are incomplete, leaving uncertainty about current deployment prevalence.
- Static repository analysis cannot establish credential-compromise probability, network segmentation, host filesystem permissions, external rate limiting, or operational disk quotas.
